--TEST--
`Core\Config::set('memory', …)` above the `[limits]` default moves the ceiling the runtime enforces, and `::restore` puts it back — ADR 0005, `rule:errors/on-limit`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The half of ADR 0005 a `false`-counting case cannot reach: an accepted set
// has to *take effect*, not merely be echoed back by `get`. What proves it is
// the allocation below — 64 MiB held live against a file that grants 16M. In a
// tree where the request's cached ceiling did not move with the overlay, this
// case does not print a wrong line, it dies at `Core\Str::repeat` with ADR
// 0020 § 1's `FATAL`, which is exactly what the sibling case
// `config-set-memory-above-the-hard-ceiling-is-refused-and-the-ceiling-in-force-stands.nvst`
// pins deliberately.
echo "default=", Core\Config::get("memory") ?? "unset", "\n";

// Under the `[limits.hard]` ceiling of 256M, so ADR 0005's bound is not what is
// being asked about here.
echo "raised=", (Core\Config::set("memory", "192M") ? "ok" : "refused"), "\n";
echo "value=", Core\Config::get("memory") ?? "unset", "\n";

// Sixty-four slabs of 1 MiB, retained: the array holds every one, so this is
// live bytes and not traffic. Four times the file's own grant and a third of
// what the set asked for.
array<string> $ballast = [];
int $slabs = 0;
while ($slabs < 64) {
    $ballast[] = Core\Str::repeat("x", 1048576);
    $slabs++;
}
echo "held=", Core\Arr::count($ballast), "\n";

// And the ceiling moves back down, which is the same cache read the other way.
// The ballast is dropped first because it has to be: `restore` puts 16M back in
// force while 64 MiB is still live, and the next helper call would then breach a
// ceiling this request had already been allowed to exceed. That ordering is a
// property of the feature, not an awkwardness of the fixture — a request cannot
// un-allocate by lowering its own limit.
$ballast = [];
Core\Config::restore("memory");
echo "restored=", Core\Config::get("memory") ?? "unset", "\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
# The grant this case raises itself above, and the ceiling it stays under. ADR
# 0064 § 5 is the unit; `[limits] memory` is `Runtime`, so a request may move it
# freely below `[limits.hard]`.
[limits]
memory = "16M"

[limits.hard]
memory = "256M"
--EXPECT--
default=16M
raised=ok
value=192M
held=64
restored=16M
